 "summary": "ASL is the source language in which firmware describes devices and power-management control methods in ACPI tables (DSDT/SSDT); it compiles to AML, a compact bytecode interpreted by the operating system's ACPI interpreter. ASL combines a declarative object namespace (Device, Name, Package, OperationRegion, Field) with methods containing If/While/Switch, integer arithmetic and mutexes.",
 "status": "active",
 "defined_by": ["consortium-standard", "reference-implementation"],
 "standards": ["ACPI Specification 6.5 (UEFI Forum)"],
 "file_extensions": [".asl", ".dsl", ".aml"],
 "homepage": "https://uefi.org/specifications",
 "influenced_by": ["c"],
 "related": [{"id": "devicetree", "relation": "family"}],
 "level_notes": {"0": "ACPICA (Intel's ACPI Component Architecture) is the reference implementation: the iASL compiler/disassembler and the AML interpreter used by Linux, FreeBSD and others. Windows has its own interpreter.", "1": "Chapters 19 (ASL reference) and 20 (AML specification, with grammar and byte encoding) of the ACPI specification are normative; chapter 5 gives the namespace and control-method model.", "3": "Covered within the Wikipedia ACPI article; OSDev wiki AML pages report blocked."},
